window.__courseChapter("v1-02", {"v1-02-00-overview": {"title": "Overview: the big ideas", "summary": "The chapter's key concepts and how its sections fit together.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Purpose", "concept map"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "Welcome to **System Paradigms** (Vol I). This overview gives you the chapter's big ideas before the section-by-section lessons. Read the claims once, flip the flashcards, then take the warm-up quiz: it is fine to miss questions here, the lessons that follow fill the gaps."}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, Purpose", "md": "The defining insight of ML systems engineering is that constraints drive architecture. The speed of light sets an absolute floor on how quickly distant servers can respond, thermodynamics limits how much computation can occur in a given volume before heat becomes unmanageable, and memory physics makes moving data more expensive than processing it."}, {"type": "flow", "title": "The path through this chapter", "nodes": [{"label": "Four Deployment Regimes Across Nine Orde", "detail": "Lesson 1 covers this section."}, {"label": "Physical Constraints on Deployment", "detail": "Lesson 2 covers this section."}, {"label": "Analyzing Workloads", "detail": "Lesson 3 covers this section."}, {"label": "System Balance and Hardware", "detail": "Lesson 4 covers this section."}, {"label": "Cloud ML: Computational Power", "detail": "Lesson 5 covers this section."}, {"label": "Edge ML: Latency and Privacy", "detail": "Lesson 6 covers this section."}, {"label": "Mobile ML: Offline Intelligence", "detail": "Lesson 7 covers this section."}]}, {"type": "table", "headers": ["Concept", "The claim to remember"], "rows": [["**Silicon contract**", "Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated."], ["**Workload archetypes and lighthouses**", "Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it."], ["**Physical walls set deployment tiers**", "The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory."], ["**Device energy budgets**", "On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally."], ["**Data locality invariant**", "A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time."], ["**Screen then cost**", "Choosing where to run applies successive filters, first whether ML beats a simpler baseline (the complexity tax), then privacy, latency, resource, and cost checks, comparing cost only among survivors on matched lifecycle budgets, and an empty set forces redesign or partitioning."], ["**Hybrid partitioning**", "When no single tier satisfies a whole pipeline, stages go where their limiting term is cheapest through train-serve splits, hierarchical filtering and confidence cascades, and progressive deployment, at the price of keeping features, versions, and preprocessing synchronized across tiers."], ["**Memory wall**", "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier."], ["**Bottleneck principle**", "In a pipelined system throughput is set by the slowest stage and overlapped stages cost the maximum of their times, so effort on any stage but the slowest one buys nothing."], ["**Data gravity**", "Shipping data over a network grows with data volume while shipping the computation does not, so large datasets pull computation to where the data lives."], ["**Total cost of ownership**", "Comparing deployment paradigms needs the total cost of ownership, the upfront capital plus recurring operating expense (power, cooling, egress, storage, labor) over the system's lifetime, because hourly accelerator rates hide most of the bill and low utilization makes fixed pools expensive."]]}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "key", "title": "Formulas you will meet", "md": "$$\\text{Latency}_{\\min} = \\frac{2 \\times \\text{Distance}}{c_{\\text{fiber}}} \\approx \\frac{2 \\times \\text{Distance}}{200{,}000 \\text{ km/s}}$$\n\n$$\\text{Power} \\propto C \\times V^2 \\times f \\quad \\text{where } V \\propto f \\implies \\text{Power} \\propto f^3$$\n\n$$\\frac{\\text{Compute Growth Rate}}{\\text{Memory Bandwidth Growth Rate}} \\approx \\frac{1.6}{1.2} \\approx 1.33$$\n\n$$T = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$$"}, {"type": "flashcards", "id": "v1-02-cards-concepts", "title": "Key concepts", "cards": [{"front": "Silicon contract", "back": "Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated."}, {"front": "Workload archetypes and lighthouses", "back": "Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it."}, {"front": "Physical walls set deployment tiers", "back": "The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory."}, {"front": "Device energy budgets", "back": "On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally."}, {"front": "Data locality invariant", "back": "A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time."}, {"front": "Screen then cost", "back": "Choosing where to run applies successive filters, first whether ML beats a simpler baseline (the complexity tax), then privacy, latency, resource, and cost checks, comparing cost only among survivors on matched lifecycle budgets, and an empty set forces redesign or partitioning."}, {"front": "Hybrid partitioning", "back": "When no single tier satisfies a whole pipeline, stages go where their limiting term is cheapest through train-serve splits, hierarchical filtering and confidence cascades, and progressive deployment, at the price of keeping features, versions, and preprocessing synchronized across tiers."}, {"front": "Memory wall", "back": "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier."}, {"front": "Bottleneck principle", "back": "In a pipelined system throughput is set by the slowest stage and overlapped stages cost the maximum of their times, so effort on any stage but the slowest one buys nothing."}, {"front": "Data gravity", "back": "Shipping data over a network grows with data volume while shipping the computation does not, so large datasets pull computation to where the data lives."}]}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-quiz-warmup", "title": "Warm-up: match the big ideas", "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Silicon contract**?", "options": ["The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory.", "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier.", "Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated.", "Shipping data over a network grows with data volume while shipping the computation does not, so large datasets pull computation to where the data lives."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Silicon contract:** Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Silicon contract itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Workload archetypes and lighthouses**?", "options": ["On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally.", "In a pipelined system throughput is set by the slowest stage and overlapped stages cost the maximum of their times, so effort on any stage but the slowest one buys nothing.", "Choosing where to run applies successive filters, first whether ML beats a simpler baseline (the complexity tax), then privacy, latency, resource, and cost checks, comparing cost only among survivors on matched lifecycle budgets, and an empty set forces redesign or partitioning.", "Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Workload archetypes and lighthouses:** Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Workload archetypes and lighthouses itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Physical walls set deployment tiers**?", "options": ["Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it.", "On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally.", "Comparing deployment paradigms needs the total cost of ownership, the upfront capital plus recurring operating expense (power, cooling, egress, storage, labor) over the system's lifetime, because hourly accelerator rates hide most of the bill and low utilization makes fixed pools expensive.", "The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory."], "answer": 3, "explanation": "**Physical walls set deployment tiers:** The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Physical walls set deployment tiers itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Device energy budgets**?", "options": ["Comparing deployment paradigms needs the total cost of ownership, the upfront capital plus recurring operating expense (power, cooling, egress, storage, labor) over the system's lifetime, because hourly accelerator rates hide most of the bill and low utilization makes fixed pools expensive.", "On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally.", "Choosing where to run applies successive filters, first whether ML beats a simpler baseline (the complexity tax), then privacy, latency, resource, and cost checks, comparing cost only among survivors on matched lifecycle budgets, and an empty set forces redesign or partitioning.", "Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it."], "answer": 1, "explanation": "**Device energy budgets:** On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Device energy budgets itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Data locality invariant**?", "options": ["When no single tier satisfies a whole pipeline, stages go where their limiting term is cheapest through train-serve splits, hierarchical filtering and confidence cascades, and progressive deployment, at the price of keeping features, versions, and preprocessing synchronized across tiers.", "The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory.", "A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time.", "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Data locality invariant:** A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Data locality invariant itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["Silicon contract: Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated.", "Workload archetypes and lighthouses: Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it.", "Physical walls set deployment tiers: The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory.", "Device energy budgets: On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Silicon contract", "def": "Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated."}, {"term": "Workload archetypes and lighthouses", "def": "Classifying a workload by what limits it first gives four archetypes, compute-bound (ResNet-50 training), bandwidth-bound (batch-one LLM decode), capacity-bound (DLRM embeddings), and power-bound (keyword spotting), and the class is a regime, not a model property, since batch, precision, or hardware can move a model across it."}, {"term": "Physical walls set deployment tiers", "def": "The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory."}, {"term": "Device energy budgets", "def": "On untethered devices energy per inference is first-order with two distinct limits, a battery budget fixable by duty cycling or a lighter model and a thermal ceiling on sustained power that throttles regardless of charge, and transmitting raw data can cost more energy than inferring locally."}, {"term": "Data locality invariant", "def": "A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time."}, {"term": "Screen then cost", "def": "Choosing where to run applies successive filters, first whether ML beats a simpler baseline (the complexity tax), then privacy, latency, resource, and cost checks, comparing cost only among survivors on matched lifecycle budgets, and an empty set forces redesign or partitioning."}, {"term": "Hybrid partitioning", "def": "When no single tier satisfies a whole pipeline, stages go where their limiting term is cheapest through train-serve splits, hierarchical filtering and confidence cascades, and progressive deployment, at the price of keeping features, versions, and preprocessing synchronized across tiers."}, {"term": "Memory wall", "def": "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier."}, {"term": "Bottleneck principle", "def": "In a pipelined system throughput is set by the slowest stage and overlapped stages cost the maximum of their times, so effort on any stage but the slowest one buys nothing."}, {"term": "Data gravity", "def": "Shipping data over a network grows with data volume while shipping the computation does not, so large datasets pull computation to where the data lives."}, {"term": "Total cost of ownership", "def": "Comparing deployment paradigms needs the total cost of ownership, the upfront capital plus recurring operating expense (power, cooling, egress, storage, labor) over the system's lifetime, because hourly accelerator rates hide most of the bill and low utilization makes fixed pools expensive."}], "id": "v1-02-00-overview", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 10}, "v1-02-01-four-deployment-regimes-across-n": {"title": "Four Deployment Regimes Across Nine Orders of Magnitude", "summary": "Compare the operational power and memory envelopes across the four deployment paradigms.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Four Deployment Regimes Across Nine Orders of Magnitude"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the operational power and memory envelopes across the four deployment paradigms\n- Explain the concept of the silicon contract and how physical hardware constraints define system performance limits\n- Justify why deployment must be treated as a first-order D·A·M co-design problem rather than a late-stage packaging task"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Four Deployment Regimes Across Nine Orders of Magnitude", "md": "A wake-word detector on a smartwatch operates inside milliwatt power budgets and kilobyte memory limits, whereas a recommendation engine in a data center draws on terabytes of embedding tables and megawatt-scale infrastructure. The two sit at opposite ends of the range of machines that run ML. The introduction's D·A·M taxonomy (a later section) treated Data, Algorithm, and Machine as three interacting dimensions of one design problem, and across real deployments the machine is the dimension that changes most sharply. Its physical limits decide which data and algorithmic choices remain open."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "Pay attention to: Four deployment paradigms across nine orders of magnitude in power and memory; The single-node stack architecture and the silicon contract; D·A·M taxonomy framing of deployment as a first-order co-design problem."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does deploying an ML model across different paradigms require treating deployment as a first-order D·A·M co-design problem rather than a late-stage packaging step?", "md": "Deployment is a first-order co-design problem because physical machine constraints (such as power, thermal dissipation, memory bandwidth, and on-chip SRAM capacity) dictate what algorithmic structures and data representations are feasible. Deferring deployment until after training often results in models that exceed target memory budgets or violate latency SLAs, requiring fundamental redesigns of the model architecture, precision formats, or batching strategies."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-01-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Which statement correctly characterizes the span of power and memory capacity across the four deployment paradigms (Cloud ML, Edge ML, Mobile ML, and TinyML)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Cloud ML operates at megawatt (\\$10^6\\text{ W}$) scale in data centers with terabytes (\\$10^{12}\\text{ bytes}$) of memory, whereas TinyML operates on microcontrollers consuming milliwatts (\\$10^{-3}\\text{ W}$) with kilobytes (\\$10^3\\text{ bytes}$) of RAM, establishing a nine-order-of-magnitude span in power and memory capacity. The option claiming a three-order power span severely underestimates data-center power and microcontroller power limits. The claim that Edge, Mobile, and TinyML have identical sub-5 W power ignores that edge servers typically consume 100 W to several hundred watts. The option asserting only a \\$10^3$ memory variation fails to recognize the gap between terabytes and kilobytes (\\$10^9$ ratio).", "type": "single", "options": ["Power spans approximately nine orders of magnitude from megawatts in cloud data centers to milliwatts in microcontrollers, while memory spans from terabytes to kilobytes.", "Power spans three orders of magnitude from kilowatts to watts, while memory remains in the gigabyte range across all four deployment tiers.", "Power is identical across Edge, Mobile, and TinyML tiers (all operating under 5 W), with only Cloud ML exceeding 100 W.", "Memory capacity varies by at most a factor of \\$10^3$ between cloud servers and microcontrollers, while power varies by \\$10^{12}$."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: compare the operational power and memory envelopes across the four deployment paradigms."]}, {"q": "In ML systems engineering, what is meant by the **silicon contract** established by a single-node stack?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. The silicon contract refers to the fixed performance bargain a specific piece of hardware provides to a model, dictated by its physical limits: peak arithmetic rate ($R_{\\text{peak}}$), memory bandwidth ($\\text{BW}$), and fixed execution overhead ($L_{\\text{lat}}$). Software can only optimize within this boundary. The option referring to legal foundry warranties confuses systems architectural concepts with commercial guarantees. The choice describing an automated portability abstraction conflates compiler frameworks with hardware limits. The claim regarding exceeding thermal limits indefinitely violates basic thermodynamics.", "type": "single", "options": ["The legal warranty provided by chip foundries guaranteeing zero hardware defects during multi-week training runs.", "The fixed performance bargain offered by a hardware platform, defined by its peak compute rate, memory bandwidth, and fixed launch overhead.", "The software abstraction layer that guarantees automated portability across CPUs, GPUs, and microcontrollers without performance regression.", "The dynamic power-scaling policy that allows an accelerator to exceed its thermal design power indefinitely under heavy batch loads."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: explain the concept of the silicon contract and how physical hardware constraints define system performance limits."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["**Memory capacity**: The amount of resident model and runtime state a machine can hold; fitting is a feasibility condition before speed is considered."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Memory capacity", "def": "The amount of resident model and runtime state a machine can hold; fitting is a feasibility condition before speed is considered."}], "id": "v1-02-01-four-deployment-regimes-across-n", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-02-physical-constraints-on-deployme": {"title": "Physical Constraints on Deployment", "summary": "Calculate the physical latency floor imposed by the speed of light in optical fiber for distributed ML workloads.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Physical Constraints on Deployment", "The light barrier", "The power wall", "The memory wall"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the physical latency floor imposed by the speed of light in optical fiber for distributed ML workloads\n- Explain the cubic relationship between clock frequency and dynamic power in CMOS circuits and its impact on hardware scaling\n- Analyze the quantitative divergence between processor compute growth and memory bandwidth growth that creates the memory wall\n- Evaluate the thermal throttling mechanism on mobile devices and its operational impact on sustained inference throughput\n- Explain the transistor power-scaling law whose breakdown led to domain-specific ML accelerators"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Physical Constraints on Deployment", "md": "A safety system with a 10 ms reaction budget cannot wait for a cross-country round trip, and a billion-parameter model cannot be squeezed into a microcontroller by better code alone. Both follow directly from the physical laws governing the speed of light, power thermodynamics, and memory signaling. The light barrier limits remote responsiveness, the power wall restricts sustained throughput, and the memory wall caps data transfer. Where a system runs therefore changes the terms of its silicon contract, and with them what the hardware can offer the model."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- The light barrier\n- The power wall\n- The memory wall\n\nPay attention to: The light barrier and speed-of-light propagation latency floors; The power wall, cubic power scaling, and Dennard scaling breakdown; The memory wall and the compounding divergence between compute and bandwidth growth."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: What causes thermal throttling on passively cooled mobile devices running sustained ML inference, and how does it affect continuous model execution?", "md": "Passively cooled mobile devices have a limited thermal dissipation capacity (typically 3–5 W TDP). When sustained ML computation generates heat faster than the chassis can dissipate it, the internal temperature rises until it reaches the hardware thermal trip point (e.g., 80 °C). The operating system then throttles clock frequencies and duty cycles to protect the hardware, which can cause frame rates and inference throughput to drop precipitously (e.g., from 60 FPS down to 15 FPS)."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-02-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A safety-critical autonomous braking system requires an end-to-end decision latency budget of 10 ms. A cloud data center is located 1,500 km away. Given that light travels through optical fiber at approximately \\$200,000 km/s, why is offloading inference to this cloud data center physically impossible?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The minimum round-trip propagation time over optical fiber is $\\frac{2 \\times 1{,}500\\text{ km}}{200{,}000\\text{ km/s}} = 0.015\\text{ s} = 15\\text{ ms}$. This propagation delay alone exceeds the 10 ms budget by 5 ms before accounting for network routing, packet queuing, or model inference. The option citing a fixed 25 ms server queue latency confuses operational queuing with an invariant physical floor. The option claiming a 5 ms round trip miscalculates the distance-speed formula. The option attributing the failure to wireless handshakes references protocol overhead rather than the primary speed-of-light constraint.", "type": "single", "options": ["The cloud server queue latency always adds at least 25 ms regardless of server load.", "The speed of light in fiber allows a round trip in 5 ms, but serialization of model weights requires 15 ms.", "The minimum speed-of-light round-trip propagation time alone is 15 ms, consuming the entire 10 ms budget before compute begins.", "Wireless transmission protocols require a minimum 50 ms handshake before transmitting sensor packets."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the physical latency floor imposed by the speed of light in optical fiber for distributed ML workloads."]}, {"q": "Under dynamic CMOS power scaling where supply voltage scales proportionally with clock frequency ($V \\propto f$), doubling the processor clock frequency increases dynamic power dissipation by a factor of 8 ($f^3$).", "explanation": "True. Dynamic CMOS power follows Power propto C × V^2 × f. When supply voltage V must scale proportionally with frequency f to support faster switching, substituting V propto f yields Power propto C × f^3. Consequently, doubling clock frequency (\\$2×) results in an \\$8× (\\$2^3) increase in dynamic power, which explains why frequency scaling stalled when Dennard scaling broke down.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: explain the cubic relationship between clock frequency and dynamic power in CMOS circuits and its impact on hardware scaling."]}, {"q": "How does the memory wall manifest in modern computing hardware according to the historical growth rates of compute capacity versus memory bandwidth?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. The memory wall is defined by the widening gap between compute growth (doubling roughly every 18 months, ~60% annual growth) and memory bandwidth growth (~20% annual growth), yielding a growth factor ratio of approximately $\\frac{1.6}{1.2} \\approx 1.33$. This divergence makes data movement an increasingly dominant bottleneck across successive hardware generations. The option reversing compute and memory bandwidth growth contradicts historical scaling trends. The option claiming identical growth rates ignores the core definition of the memory wall. The option claiming memory signaling energy has dropped to near zero contradicts the physics of memory bus signaling.", "type": "single", "options": ["Memory bandwidth doubles every 18 months while compute throughput increases by only 20% annually, causing compute starvation.", "Both compute throughput and memory bandwidth grow at identical 60% annual rates, but memory capacity limits model size.", "Processors have reached absolute thermal limits while memory signaling energy has dropped to near-zero levels.", "Compute capacity historically doubles roughly every 18 months (~60% annually), whereas memory bandwidth grows by only ~20% annually, causing the compute-to-bandwidth gap to widen compounding over time."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the quantitative divergence between processor compute growth and memory bandwidth growth that creates the memory wall."]}, {"q": "The historical scaling relationship under which shrinking MOSFET transistor dimensions allowed reducing voltage and current while keeping power density constant is known as ____ scaling.", "explanation": "Dennard. Dennard scaling (formulated by Robert Dennard in 1974) held that as transistors shrank, power density remained constant because operating voltage and current scaled downward proportionally. Its breakdown in the mid-2000s ended free clock frequency scaling and forced the computing industry toward multicore parallelism and domain-specific accelerators.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["Dennard"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the transistor power-scaling law whose breakdown led to domain-specific ML accelerators."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the physical latency floor imposed by the speed of light in optical fiber for distributed ML workloads.", "You can now explain the cubic relationship between clock frequency and dynamic power in CMOS circuits and its impact on hardware scaling.", "You can now analyze the quantitative divergence between processor compute growth and memory bandwidth growth that creates the memory wall.", "You can now evaluate the thermal throttling mechanism on mobile devices and its operational impact on sustained inference throughput."]}], "id": "v1-02-02-physical-constraints-on-deployme", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-03-analyzing-workloads": {"title": "Analyzing Workloads", "summary": "Apply the iron law and bottleneck principle to distinguish single-request serialized latency from steady-state pipelined completion interval.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Analyzing Workloads", "The bottleneck principle", "Workload archetypes"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the iron law and bottleneck principle to distinguish single-request serialized latency from steady-state pipelined completion interval\n- Classify ML workloads into archetypes based on their dominant iron law bottlenecks and reference lighthouse models\n- Explain why improving a nonlimiting stage can lower sequential request latency without increasing ideal pipelined throughput\n- Sequence a hierarchical feasibility screen for embedded deployment and distinguish flash screening from separate SRAM/runtime-memory validation\n- Classify ML workloads exhibiting high arithmetic intensity into the Compute-bound archetype"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Analyzing Workloads", "md": "Which of the three physical walls a system hits first is not a fixed property of a model family. A vision network that saturates accelerator compute units during batched training can stall on memory bandwidth when serving one image at a time, and a model that runs effortlessly in a data center can overheat a phone in under a minute."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- The bottleneck principle\n- Workload archetypes\n\nPay attention to: Additive vs. max form of the iron law and bottleneck principle; The energy of wireless transmission vs. local computation; Four workload archetypes and five reference lighthouse models."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Explain why shortening a stage that is not the bottleneck can still reduce the latency of one sequential request, even though it does not improve ideal pipelined throughput once the pipeline is full.", "md": "Shortening any stage on a sequential request's dependent path reduces that request's total latency because the additive form sums all required stages. In a pipelined stream, however, ideal steady-state throughput is set by the slowest overlapped stage, so making a faster nonlimiting stage even shorter does not change the completion interval after the pipeline fills. For example, reducing compute time helps a single request finish sooner, but if memory or $T_{\\text{IO}}$ remains the longest stage, the assembly-line rate stays unchanged. The practical consequence is that engineers should separate latency optimization for one request from throughput optimization for a continuous stream."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-03-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "When analyzing ML system performance, when should an engineer apply the additive form of the iron law ($T = \\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}} + \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}} + L_{\\text{lat}}$) versus the bottleneck principle's max form ($T_{\\text{pipelined}} \\ge \\max\\left(\\frac{D_{\\text{vol}}}{\\text{BW}}, \\frac{O}{R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}}}, T_{\\text{IO}}\\right) + L_{\\text{lat}}$)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The additive form models one request's serialized latency when data movement, compute, and fixed overhead lie on the dependent path and therefore add. The max form is for pipelined steady-state throughput: after the pipeline fills, the time between completed tasks is at least the slowest overlapped stage, with $L_{\\text{lat}}$ added separately because nonoverlapped overhead sits outside the $\\max(\\cdot)$. The cloud-versus-TinyML answer confuses execution structure with deployment location, and the batch-size rule is not the governing distinction.", "type": "single", "options": ["Use the additive form for single-request serialized latency, and the max form for steady-state pipelined completion interval after the pipeline fills, where overlapped stages are bounded by the slowest one.", "Use the additive form exclusively for cloud workloads, and the max form exclusively for microcontroller TinyML workloads.", "Use the additive form when batch size is greater than 128, and the max form when batch size equals 1.", "Use the additive form during model training only, and the max form during on-device model quantization."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: apply the iron law and bottleneck principle to distinguish single-request serialized latency from steady-state pipelined completion interval."]}, {"q": "Which pairing correctly matches a workload archetype to its dominant physical bottleneck and canonical lighthouse model?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Bandwidth-bound workloads, exemplified by autoregressive language models such as GPT-2 and Llama, perform sequential token decoding where generating each token requires streaming large parameter matrices through memory, making memory bandwidth ($\\text{BW}$) the primary bottleneck. The option pairing Compute-bound with DLRM confuses dense arithmetic with sparse embedding lookups. The option pairing Capacity-bound with ResNet-50 misclassifies a dense convolution model as an irregular lookup table. The option pairing Power-bound with petabyte-scale distributed training contradicts the kilobyte memory and milliwatt power constraints of TinyML.", "type": "single", "options": ["Compute-bound rightarrow dominated by irregular table lookups and cache misses rightarrow DLRM", "Bandwidth-bound rightarrow dominated by dense parameter streaming during autoregressive token decode rightarrow GPT-2/Llama", "Capacity-bound rightarrow dominated by dense FLOP throughput at high arithmetic intensity rightarrow ResNet-50", "Power-bound rightarrow dominated by petabyte-scale distributed training rightarrow Keyword Spotting (KWS)"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: classify ML workloads into archetypes based on their dominant iron law bottlenecks and reference lighthouse models."]}, {"q": "Order the steps of the hierarchical deployment feasibility evaluation for the Smart Doorbell scenario from initial problem framing to latency verification:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) Define the application mission targets (person at the door, required quality, and responsiveness budget), (4) Select a candidate platform and model for the task (for example, a visual wake-word model on an ESP32-S3), (1) Check Level 1 storage fit by screening whether the visual wake-word model's weights fit in flash, (3) Check Level 2 timing constraints by evaluating per-inference latency against the interaction budget. The sequence is necessary because the budget and task define what \"fit\" means, the candidate must be chosen before it can be screened, and the scorecard checks flash before latency. Passing the flash check does not prove complete memory fit: SRAM for activations and runtime state still has to be checked separately.", "type": "order", "items": ["Check Level 1 storage fit by screening whether the visual wake-word model's weights fit in flash", "Check Level 2 timing constraints by evaluating per-inference latency against the interaction budget"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence a hierarchical feasibility screen for embedded deployment and distinguish flash screening from separate SRAM/runtime-memory validation."]}, {"q": "An ML workload characterized by high arithmetic intensity where throughput is bounded by peak FLOP/s ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) rather than memory bandwidth, such as dense convolutional neural network training, belongs to the ____ archetype.", "explanation": "Compute-bound. Compute-bound workloads perform many floating-point operations per byte of memory loaded ($I = \\text{FLOPs/byte}$), ensuring that processor arithmetic units remain saturated and execution time is dominated by the compute term ($O / (R_{\\text{peak}} \\cdot \\eta_{\\text{hw}})$) of the iron law.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["Compute bound", "Compute-bound"], "hints": ["Think about: classify ML workloads exhibiting high arithmetic intensity into the Compute-bound archetype."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the iron law and bottleneck principle to distinguish single-request serialized latency from steady-state pipelined completion interval.", "You can now classify ML workloads into archetypes based on their dominant iron law bottlenecks and reference lighthouse models.", "You can now explain why improving a nonlimiting stage can lower sequential request latency without increasing ideal pipelined throughput.", "You can now sequence a hierarchical feasibility screen for embedded deployment and distinguish flash screening from separate SRAM/runtime-memory validation."]}], "id": "v1-02-03-analyzing-workloads", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-04-system-balance-and-hardware": {"title": "System Balance and Hardware", "summary": "Analyze why single-batch inference is memory-bound across heterogeneous hardware platforms using the roofline model.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["System Balance and Hardware", "Inside one machine: The single-node stack", "Matching workloads to hardware"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze why single-batch inference is memory-bound across heterogeneous hardware platforms using the roofline model\n- Explain how the dominant bottleneck shifts between compute-bound and memory-bound regimes across training and inference phases\n- Apply the critical path latency rule to evaluate end-to-end ML service level agreements\n- Calculate the hardware balance point and use it to classify workloads into compute-bound and memory-bound regimes\n- Identify the dominant iron-law term for a deployment tier and the optimizations it rewards"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § System Balance and Hardware", "md": "A model that clears its latency budget on paper can still stall on a real host if transfers over PCIe, the expansion bus that connects the CPU to the GPU, choke the pipeline or if single-sample inference leaves the accelerator's matrix units starved for bytes. Checking the iron law and workload archetypes against real hardware means tracing a request through every layer of one host, from the application's latency or throughput target down to the silicon that must meet it."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Inside one machine: The single-node stack\n- Matching workloads to hardware\n\nPay attention to: Critical-path latency rule and the dominant iron-law term per deployment tier; Roofline analysis and arithmetic intensity vs. hardware balance point; Batch=1 memory-bound behavior of ResNet-50 across cloud accelerators and mobile NPUs."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does a convolutional neural network (e.g., ResNet-50) shift from a compute-bound regime during cloud training to a memory-bound regime during single-sample ($\\text{batch}=1$) edge inference?", "md": "During cloud training with large batch sizes (e.g., $\\text{batch}=256$), loaded model weights are reused across hundreds of input samples in parallel, raising arithmetic intensity ($I = \\text{FLOPs/byte}$) above the hardware balance point into the compute-bound regime. During single-sample inference ($\\text{batch}=1$), the full set of model weights must be streamed from memory to process just one input without reuse, causing arithmetic intensity to plummet onto the memory-bandwidth-bound slope of the roofline."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-04-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why is single-image (batch=1) ResNet-50 inference memory-bound on both a high-end cloud accelerator (NVIDIA A100) and a mobile NPU (iPhone 15 Pro)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. For single-image inference ($\\text{batch}=1$), the full model weight parameters must be fetched from memory to process a single sample. Because weights cannot be amortized across multiple samples in parallel, the effective arithmetic intensity ($I = \\text{FLOPs/byte}$) is low and falls to the left of the roofline ridge point ($R_{\\text{peak}}/\\text{BW}$) on both the A100 and the mobile NPU, making memory bandwidth the limiting constraint on both chips. The option citing dynamic control flow is incorrect because ResNet-50 consists of regular static feedforward convolutions. The claim that both platforms lack matrix acceleration is false, as both feature specialized tensor cores / NPUs. The option claiming activations exceed memory capacity is false, as ResNet-50 activations at batch=1 require only a few megabytes.", "type": "single", "options": ["ResNet-50 contains dynamic control flow that causes branching stalls on SIMD execution units.", "Both platforms lack matrix multiplication coprocessors, forcing scalar CPU execution.", "At batch=1, model weights must be loaded from memory once per single image without weight reuse across batch items, keeping arithmetic intensity below each platform's hardware balance point ($R_{\\text{peak}}/\\text{BW}$).", "The activations at batch=1 exceed the total high-bandwidth memory (HBM) and SRAM capacities of both devices."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: analyze why single-batch inference is memory-bound across heterogeneous hardware platforms using the roofline model."]}, {"q": "An ML service SLA guarantees a total end-to-end response time of 100 ms. The pipeline involves client preprocessing, a cross-region network RPC, model inference, and postprocessing. According to critical path latency principles, why will this system fail if a cross-region network call taking 120 ms is placed on the synchronous critical path?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. In a synchronous pipeline, end-to-end latency is bounded below by the sum of sequential operations on the critical path. If an individual operation on the critical path requires 120 ms, the system latency is guaranteed to be at least 120 ms, violating the 100 ms budget before accounting for inference, preprocessing, or postprocessing. The options citing compiler lowering errors, tensor precision corruption, or halved accelerator throughput invent non-existent technical failures instead of applying critical-path analysis.", "type": "single", "options": ["The ML framework compiler will refuse to lower the graph if network RPC operations are detected.", "Cross-region RPCs corrupt the numerical precision of FP16 tensors during serialization.", "A cross-region network call reduces the accelerator compute throughput ($R_{\\text{peak}}$) by half.", "The critical path is the longest sequential chain of dependent operations; any single stage exceeding 100 ms guarantees the end-to-end SLA will be breached regardless of how fast all other stages execute."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: apply the critical path latency rule to evaluate end-to-end ML service level agreements."]}, {"q": "For batch-1 LLM decode in the cloud and for most edge inference, the iron-law term that dominates is memory ____ rather than peak compute, so a faster accelerator alone yields little speedup.", "explanation": "bandwidth. These workloads stream weights and activations with little reuse per byte loaded, so the data-movement term $D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$ dominates. The productive optimizations reduce bytes moved (smaller models, lower precision, more reuse per loaded weight) rather than adding peak FLOP/s.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["bandwidth"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the dominant iron-law term for a deployment tier and the optimizations it rewards."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: What is the hardware balance point (or roofline ridge point) of an accelerator, and what does it tell an ML systems engineer about whether a workload will be compute-bound or memory-bound?", "md": "The hardware balance point is the ratio of peak arithmetic throughput to memory bandwidth ($R_{\\text{peak}} / \\text{BW}$), expressed in $\\text{FLOPs/byte}$. If a workload's arithmetic intensity ($I = \\text{operations} / \\text{bytes moved}$) is greater than this balance point ($I > R_{\\text{peak}}/\\text{BW}$), the workload is compute-bound and limited by processor arithmetic capacity. If $I < R_{\\text{peak}}/\\text{BW}$, the workload is memory-bound and limited by memory bandwidth, meaning buying more peak FLOP/s will provide zero speedup unless data movement is reduced."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze why single-batch inference is memory-bound across heterogeneous hardware platforms using the roofline model.", "You can now explain how the dominant bottleneck shifts between compute-bound and memory-bound regimes across training and inference phases.", "You can now apply the critical path latency rule to evaluate end-to-end ML service level agreements.", "You can now calculate the hardware balance point and use it to classify workloads into compute-bound and memory-bound regimes."]}], "id": "v1-02-04-system-balance-and-hardware", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-05-cloud-ml-computational-power": {"title": "Cloud ML: Computational Power", "summary": "Evaluate the listed edge-server budget to identify the dominant role of operational labor.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Cloud ML: Computational Power", "Cloud infrastructure and scale", "Cloud ML trade-offs and constraints"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Evaluate the listed edge-server budget to identify the dominant role of operational labor\n- Explain the data-gravity principle and its implications for data-movement costs in cloud systems\n- Classify the operational and legal boundaries of data erasure regulations (GDPR) on deployed ML models"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Cloud ML: Computational Power", "md": "At the top of that physical gradient sits Cloud ML, which pools power, cooling, and accelerators in centralized data centers to serve workloads that no local machine can hold. The estimated resources needed to train GPT-3 show why this tier exists: … of computation, equivalent to … running for approximately … at the reported sustained rate, and about … of electricity."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Cloud infrastructure and scale\n- Cloud ML trade-offs and constraints\n\nPay attention to: Centralized data center scale and training throughput; The distance penalty, GDPR/HIPAA compliance, and Cloud vs. Edge TCO; The Voice Assistant Wall and infrastructure/economic limits of pure cloud processing."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: What is the data-gravity principle in cloud ML, and why does it make moving multi-terabyte raw sensor datasets to the cloud economically and physically problematic?", "md": "The data-gravity principle states that as data volume ($D_{\\text{vol}}$) grows, the time and cost of moving the data to remote compute eventually exceed the cost of moving the computation (a model and its code) to the data, because transfer time grows with the data while shipping the computation costs roughly the same however much data waits. For massive sensor streams (such as hundreds of raw video feeds generating tens of gigabytes per second), the raw upload can exceed uplink capacity; uplink service and storage costs may also matter, while cloud egress applies to later outbound transfers rather than the upload itself. Local computation can reduce transport, but compressed frames, features, or event summaries may also change the comparison. Evaluate the transmitted representation and complete local and remote paths before choosing placement."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-05-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In the chapter's 3-year budget comparison for an on-premises edge server deployment serving 1 million inferences per day, what is the largest component of the edge system's listed annual budget in the chapter's scenario?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. In the chapter's illustrative edge-server budget, engineering labor (e.g., 0.1 FTE DevOps support per server/site, totaling ~\\$15,000/year) accounts for roughly 69% of the listed annual edge budget, far exceeding hardware CapEx (~\\$5,000/year), power (~\\$315/year), or cooling (~\\$95/year). Teams that focus solely on minimizing hardware purchase costs systematically underestimate the total cost of ownership. The choices identifying hardware amortization, electricity, or cooling as the dominant cost do not match this scenario. The comparison is a partial budget: cloud operating labor and edge redundancy are not priced, so it does not establish a matched total-cost ranking.", "type": "single", "options": ["GPU hardware silicon purchase price amortized over 3 years", "DevOps engineering labor required to maintain, update, and manage on-premises edge infrastructure", "Electricity costs for running the server 24/7", "Dedicated server room air-conditioning and cooling overhead"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the listed edge-server budget to identify the dominant role of operational labor."]}, {"q": "Under GDPR Article 17 (Right to Erasure), when a user requests deletion of their data, an organization is legally mandated to retrain from scratch every ML model that ever encountered that user's training samples.", "explanation": "False. GDPR Article 17 provides a right to erasure across source data stores, derived feature pipelines, caches, and logs where specified grounds apply, but it is technology-neutral and does not prescribe retraining every trained model as the sole mandatory remediation. Organizations evaluate risk, data persistence, and technical measures appropriate to the system.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: classify the operational and legal boundaries of data erasure regulations (GDPR) on deployed ML models."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now evaluate the listed edge-server budget to identify the dominant role of operational labor.", "You can now explain the data-gravity principle and its implications for data-movement costs in cloud systems.", "You can now classify the operational and legal boundaries of data erasure regulations (GDPR) on deployed ML models."]}], "id": "v1-02-05-cloud-ml-computational-power", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-06-edge-ml-latency-and-privacy": {"title": "Edge ML: Latency and Privacy", "summary": "Calculate aggregate sensor bandwidth demand and compare it against uplink limits to diagnose when edge processing is mandatory.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Edge ML: Latency and Privacy", "The data locality invariant: When the network is too slow", "Edge ML deployment challenges", "Real-time industrial and IoT systems"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate aggregate sensor bandwidth demand and compare it against uplink limits to diagnose when edge processing is mandatory\n- Apply the data locality invariant equation to determine whether an application must run locally at the edge\n- Evaluate why high-bandwidth cellular networks (5G/6G) cannot eliminate the need for edge ML in sub-10 ms control loops\n- Calculate sustained accelerator requirements for edge deployments using hardware derating factors\n- Evaluate the control loop timing constraints governing real-time industrial IoT ML deployments"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Edge ML: Latency and Privacy", "md": "The robotic-arm safety monitor in napkin math \\ref{nbk-ml-systems-distance-penalty}, an industrial IoT (IIoT) control loop, left one option open: running the model beside the arm it protects. Edge ML is built on that move, trading elastic cloud capacity for shorter request paths and local data retention."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- The data locality invariant: When the network is too slow\n- Edge ML deployment challenges\n- Real-time industrial and IoT systems\n\nPay attention to: Bandwidth bottleneck in local sensor aggregation vs. cloud streaming; The data locality invariant and remote deadline feasibility testing; Edge accelerator sizing, hardware derating, and real-time industrial IoT."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why cannot high-bandwidth wireless networking technologies (such as 5G or 6G) eliminate the need for edge ML in tight real-time control loops ($<10\\text{ ms}$)?", "md": "While 5G and 6G technologies dramatically increase network bandwidth ($\\text{BW}_{\\text{network}}$) and reduce data transmission serialization time, they cannot alter the speed of light in optical fiber or eliminate physical geographic routing delays and protocol overhead ($L_{\\text{lat,network}}$). For tight real-time control loops ($<10\\text{ ms}$), physical round-trip propagation over geographic distances alone can consume the entire latency budget before any computation begins, making local edge execution physically mandatory."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-06-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A manufacturing facility deploys 100 cameras recording uncompressed 1080p video (\\$1920 × 1080 × 3 bytes/pixel) at 30 FPS. The factory has a dedicated 10 Gbps (~1.25 GB/s) fiber uplink. Why is streaming all raw video to a cloud ML server physically impossible?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Each camera produces about \\$1920 \\times 1080 \\times 3 \\times 30 \\approx 186.6\\text{ MB/s}$, and multiplying by 100 cameras gives about \\$18.66\\text{ GB/s}$. The 10 Gbps uplink delivers only about \\$1.25\\text{ GB/s}$, so the raw stream is roughly \\$15\\times$ larger than the available link. The per-camera answer fails to multiply by all 100 cameras, the gigabits-versus-gigabytes answer compares unlike units without converting, and the compression answer assumes a reduction mechanism the scenario never grants. The physical impossibility here is for uncompressed raw streaming under the stated link budget.", "type": "single", "options": ["The total is only about \\$186.6 MB/s because that is the per-camera rate, so the 10 Gbps uplink has ample headroom.", "The aggregate is about \\$18.7 Gb/s because the camera output should be compared numerically to 10 Gbps without converting bytes to bits, so the link is close enough to be practical.", "The aggregate raw video data rate is approximately \\$18.7 GB/s, which exceeds the \\$1.25 GB/s uplink capacity by roughly \\$15×.", "The aggregate raw stream is large, but normal compression assumptions would make it fit within the 10 Gbps uplink in practice."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate aggregate sensor bandwidth demand and compare it against uplink limits to diagnose when edge processing is mandatory."]}, {"q": "An autonomous drone navigates at 60 FPS (16.7 ms per-frame budget) capturing uncompressed 4K frames (~24.9 MB per frame). The drone connects via a 100 Mbps uplink to a cloud server whose round-trip latency plus inference time is 110 ms. Applying the data locality invariant, what is the feasibility of offloading this obstacle avoidance system to the cloud?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. Under the data locality screen, the remote-path lower-bound estimate is RemotePathTime ge fracDataVolumeNetworkBandwidth + NetworkLatency + fracOperationsRemotePeakRate × RemoteHardwareEfficiency. Transmitting \\$24.9 MB = 199.2 Mbits over a \\$100 Mbps uplink requires frac199.2100 approx 1.992 s approx 2,000 ms. Adding the 110 ms network/compute delay yields RemotePathTime approx 1,992 + 110 approx 2,100 ms, vastly exceeding the 16.7 ms per-frame budget. Because transmission time alone exceeds the deadline, this raw-frame offload is infeasible. A lower-bound estimate below the deadline would instead require complete-path measurement, including remote memory movement, queues, preprocessing, runtime overhead, and response serialization. The options claiming feasibility ignore the two-second transmission bottleneck. The option blaming telemetry protocols misses the fundamental bandwidth-latency physics.", "type": "single", "options": ["Feasible, because the cloud GPU computes inference in under 10 ms.", "Feasible, provided 5G network slicing is enabled on the drone modem.", "Infeasible, solely because cloud servers do not support drone telemetry protocols.", "Infeasible, because transmitting a 24.9 MB frame over a 100 Mbps link requires ~2,000 ms, causing even the remote-path lower-bound estimate (~2,100 ms) to violate the 16.7 ms frame deadline by over two orders of magnitude."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: apply the data locality invariant equation to determine whether an application must run locally at the edge."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: A retail store needs about 5.2 TOPS of sustained throughput. Why does the chapter's estimate call for three 4 TOPS boards rather than two, even though two boards' combined 8 TOPS peak exceeds the target?", "md": "Hardware datasheets publish peak theoretical throughput ($R_{\\text{peak}}$), but real-world sustained workloads encounter thermal limits, memory bandwidth bottlenecks, and software overhead, so the peak rating alone does not establish sustained performance. Under the illustrative 50% derating assumption, each 4 TOPS board sustains about 2 TOPS, so the target needs 5.2 ÷ 2 ≈ 2.6 boards, which rounds up to three. Multiple boards or a higher-tier accelerator remain candidates only if the model's operators and numerical format are supported. A multi-board design additionally requires divisible work and manageable coordination overhead; measurements of the actual model and runtime must establish sustained performance and task quality."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["**Data locality**: Placing computation near the data when transfer delay or bandwidth makes remote execution infeasible."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Data locality", "def": "Placing computation near the data when transfer delay or bandwidth makes remote execution infeasible."}], "id": "v1-02-06-edge-ml-latency-and-privacy", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-07-mobile-ml-offline-intelligence": {"title": "Mobile ML: Offline Intelligence", "summary": "Compare the battery tax and the thermal wall as distinct energy and thermodynamic constraints in mobile ML.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Mobile ML: Offline Intelligence", "Mobile ML benefits and resource constraints", "Personal assistant and media processing"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Compare the battery tax and the thermal wall as distinct energy and thermodynamic constraints in mobile ML\n- Analyze the system scheduling and thermal constraints of multi-stage computational photography pipelines on mobile SoCs\n- Analyze why desktop-trained models fail on mobile devices due to activation memory, thermal budgets, and unsupported operations\n- Evaluate how dedicated hardware enclaves (e.g., Secure Enclave) provide biometric privacy while recognizing holistic system threat models\n- Explain the physical sequence of events during mobile thermal throttling under unoptimized compute workloads"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Mobile ML: Offline Intelligence", "md": "Carrying edge-style locality into a user's pocket changes which budget runs out first. A phone must deliver responsive, private inference all day from a pocket-sized battery, inside a thin chassis with no fan to carry heat away. Mobile ML integrates machine learning directly into smartphones and tablets to meet that envelope. It fits workloads where data locality, offline operation, and immediate response matter more than computational scale, such as voice recognition, computational photography, health monitoring, and navigation or translation that must keep working without a network."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Mobile ML benefits and resource constraints\n- Personal assistant and media processing\n\nPay attention to: Battery tax vs. thermal wall on passively cooled smartphones; Multi-stage computational photography pipeline scheduling; Pitfalls of deploying unoptimized desktop-trained models to mobile SoCs."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does deploying an unoptimized desktop-trained vision model (such as an FP32 ResNet-50 pipeline) directly onto a mobile device frequently fail, even when the model's FP32 weights (~102.2 MB) appear smaller than the phone's total RAM?", "md": "While the raw FP32 weights are ~102.2 MB, a complete desktop pipeline requires gigabytes of memory once intermediate activation tensors, input/output preprocessing buffers, runtime framework overhead, and batch buffers are allocated. Furthermore, unoptimized desktop models execute billions of FLOPs (e.g., 8.2 GFLOP per inference) using unsupported float operations or desktop-specific layers. Even where the weights fit in RAM, a low-end edge processor then takes seconds per inference, a latency failure rather than a capacity one, while the full multi-gigabyte pipeline can still cause out-of-memory crashes."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-07-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "How do the **battery tax** and the **thermal wall** differ as physical constraints on mobile ML systems?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The battery tax represents the depletion of finite electrochemical energy ($E = P \\cdot t$) over a full day, meaning a model running continuously draws down the daily battery budget. The thermal wall represents the maximum rate of heat that a phone's passive chassis can continuously dissipate (typically 3–5 W TDP). Adding a larger battery extends runtime but cannot prevent thermal throttling if the workload exceeds the thermal dissipation ceiling. The option dividing the constraints by mobile operating system is factually baseless. The option suggesting higher clock frequencies to beat the thermal wall violates the cubic power law ($\\text{Power} \\propto f^3$). The option conflating the terms with SRAM leakage and modem power misdefines both concepts.", "type": "single", "options": ["The battery tax is an energy budget problem bounding total operations over time ($\\int \\text{Power} \\cdot dt$), whereas the thermal wall is a heat dissipation ceiling bounding instantaneous sustained power ($\\text{Power} \\le 3\\text{--}5\\text{ W}$) regardless of battery size.", "The battery tax applies only to iOS devices, while the thermal wall applies only to Android devices.", "The battery tax can be resolved by lowering clock frequency, but the thermal wall requires increasing the clock frequency to finish calculations faster.", "The battery tax represents static memory leakage in SRAM, while the thermal wall represents network transmission power in 5G modems."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: compare the battery tax and the thermal wall as distinct energy and thermodynamic constraints in mobile ML."]}, {"q": "Why does deploying a mobile computational photography feature (such as Portrait Mode or Night Mode) present a pipeline scheduling challenge rather than a single-model optimization problem?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Mobile computational photography does not rely on a single isolated model; it executes a sequence of specialized real-time models (such as depth estimation, segmentation, and denoising) alongside the ISP. All stages must execute within the user's perceived shutter delay (tens to hundreds of milliseconds) while sharing the phone's tight 3–5 W thermal envelope across heterogeneous SoC cores (CPU, GPU, NPU) without triggering thermal throttling. The option claiming one network per charge cycle is absurd. The option claiming optics prevent INT8 quantization is false, as mobile vision pipelines routinely use quantized models. The option claiming ISPs reject ML tensor inputs misrepresents modern mobile SoC architectures where ISPs and NPUs share memory.", "type": "single", "options": ["Mobile cameras can only run one neural network per battery charge cycle.", "The feature executes a multi-stage pipeline (depth estimation, semantic segmentation, alignment, and HDR tone mapping) that must all complete within a perceived shutter delay while sharing a 3–5 W thermal budget across CPU, GPU, and NPU.", "Computational photography models cannot use quantized INT8 representations due to mathematical rounding artifacts in lens optics.", "Camera image signal processors (ISPs) operate asynchronously from the operating system and reject all external ML tensor inputs."], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the system scheduling and thermal constraints of multi-stage computational photography pipelines on mobile SoCs."]}, {"q": "Running biometric matching (such as Face ID) inside a dedicated on-device Secure Enclave reduces data disclosure risks by keeping mathematical representations isolated in encrypted hardware, but it does not automatically eliminate all security or privacy risks without rigorous end-to-end system design.", "explanation": "True. Hardware isolation mechanisms like the Secure Enclave restrict biometric mathematical representations to dedicated encrypted memory on-device, preventing remote transmission and unauthorized operating system access. However, overall system security and privacy still depend on the complete threat model, authentication policies, and software implementation.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate how dedicated hardware enclaves (e.g., Secure Enclave) provide biometric privacy while recognizing holistic system threat models."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["**Power budget**: The allowed rate of energy consumption, limited by battery supply and heat dissipation."]}], "terms": [{"term": "Power budget", "def": "The allowed rate of energy consumption, limited by battery supply and heat dissipation."}], "id": "v1-02-07-mobile-ml-offline-intelligence", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-08-tinyml-ubiquitous-sensing": {"title": "TinyML: Ubiquitous Sensing", "summary": "Calculate the energy scale ratio between TinyML sensing and cloud LLM queries while distinguishing it from a matched efficiency comparison.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["TinyML: Ubiquitous Sensing", "TinyML advantages and operational trade-offs", "Environmental and health monitoring"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate the energy scale ratio between TinyML sensing and cloud LLM queries while distinguishing it from a matched efficiency comparison\n- Explain how training-state memory requirements constrain on-device learning on microcontrollers\n- Explain why sub-1 mW average power can enable energy-neutral TinyML operation only when harvested supply and stored energy cover the complete device load\n- Explain the qualitative architectural discontinuities in memory and power that differentiate TinyML from mobile ML\n- Explain the operational role of intermittent computing in energy-harvesting TinyML deployments"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § TinyML: Ubiquitous Sensing", "md": "Instrumenting every pallet in a warehouse, every cable on a suspension bridge, or every beehive in an apiary means putting \"eyes and ears\" on tens of thousands to millions of physical objects. At that count, each device must cost dollars rather than hundreds of dollars and measure millimeters rather than centimeters. Smartphones are far too expensive and too large for the role; ubiquitous sensing at this scale requires unit costs under ten dollars."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- TinyML advantages and operational trade-offs\n- Environmental and health monitoring\n\nPay attention to: Eight-order-of-magnitude energy scale comparison across different tasks and accounting boundaries; Microcontroller memory constraints (kilobyte SRAM) and on-device training limits; Ambient energy harvesting and the 1 mW average power threshold."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why is the 1 mW average power threshold considered a transformative milestone in TinyML system deployment?", "md": "Crossing below about 1 mW average power can make energy-harvesting deployment feasible, but only when the harvested source covers the device's complete long-term average load and stored energy can bridge interruptions in supply. For example, a sensor that averages under this threshold may shift from frequent battery replacement toward much longer unattended operation if its solar, thermal, or RF source is reliable enough and buffering handles dark or idle periods. The practical implication is that 1 mW is not an automatic deploy-and-forget guarantee; it is a systems crossover that still depends on source variability, storage, and the full device power budget."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-08-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Comparing a dedicated TinyML keyword spotting model (~\\$10\\ \\mu\\text{J}$ per inference) to a cloud foundation model query (~\\$1\\text{ kJ}$ per inference), by approximately what factor does energy consumption per inference differ?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The energy ratio between a cloud LLM query (\\$1 kJ = 10^3 J) and an always-on TinyML keyword spotting inference (\\$10\\ muJ = 10^-5 J) is frac10^3 J10^-5 J = 10^8 = 100,000,000× (eight orders of magnitude). This is a scale comparison across different tasks and accounting boundaries, not a controlled hardware-efficiency comparison. TinyML lifetime still depends on inference cadence, sensing, communication, sleep power, and the complete device energy budget. The options citing 2 orders (\\$100×) or 4 orders (\\$10,000×) severely underestimate the energy gap between cloud data centers and microcontrollers. The option citing 14 orders (\\$10^14) overstates the ratio.", "type": "single", "options": ["Approximately \\$100× (2 orders of magnitude)", "Approximately \\$10,000× (4 orders of magnitude)", "Approximately \\$100{,}000{,}000\\times$ (\\$10^8$, 8 orders of magnitude)", "Approximately \\$10^14× (14 orders of magnitude)"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the energy scale ratio between TinyML sensing and cloud LLM queries while distinguishing it from a matched efficiency comparison."]}, {"q": "Why are microcontroller TinyML devices predominantly restricted to inference-only execution rather than full in-situ backpropagation training?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. During inference, an intermediate buffer can be reused only after its final consumer has finished; skip connections can extend that lifetime. Conventional training retains results needed for gradient calculations and adds gradients and optimizer state. Its memory demand depends on tensor shapes, model structure, checkpointing or recomputation, and which parameters are updated, rather than depth alone. Many microcontrollers cannot fit the full training state. Methods such as TinyTL reduce it by freezing weights and updating mainly biases, but constrained local adaptation is not categorically impossible. The other choices do not explain the memory requirement.", "type": "single", "options": ["Microcontrollers use flash memory which permanently locks all transistor gates against state changes.", "Backpropagation requires double-precision FP64 floating-point arithmetic which is mathematically impossible on 32-bit cores.", "The speed of light inside microcontroller silicon is too slow to support backward gradient propagation.", "Conventional training retains intermediate results needed for gradient calculations and adds gradient and optimizer state, which can exceed a microcontroller's working-memory budget."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: explain how training-state memory requirements constrain on-device learning on microcontrollers."]}, {"q": "The power management paradigm in which a battery-less or energy-harvesting microcontroller sleeps in ultra-low-power states and wakes periodically to perform inference before energy reserves deplete is called ____ computing.", "explanation": "intermittent (or duty-cycled). Intermittent computing enables microcontrollers powered by tiny batteries (like CR2032 coin cells) or ambient energy harvesters to achieve multi-year lifespans by keeping average active power consumption in the microwatt to milliwatt regime.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["intermittent", "intermittent (or duty cycled)", "intermittent (or duty-cycled)"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the operational role of intermittent computing in energy-harvesting TinyML deployments."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: Why is it a fallacy to treat TinyML as merely 'scaled-down mobile ML' that can be addressed simply by applying standard INT8 post-training quantization?", "md": "TinyML constrains both storage and working memory: model weights and code generally reside in flash, while intermediate activations and runtime buffers must fit the smaller SRAM budget. FP32-to-INT8 conversion reduces the bytes of converted values by four, but does not guarantee that the artifact fits flash or that its peak working set fits SRAM. Power, operator support, and task quality must also meet the target envelope. Appropriate architectures and memory scheduling may therefore be needed in addition to quantization."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate the energy scale ratio between TinyML sensing and cloud LLM queries while distinguishing it from a matched efficiency comparison.", "You can now explain how training-state memory requirements constrain on-device learning on microcontrollers.", "You can now explain why sub-1 mW average power can enable energy-neutral TinyML operation only when harvested supply and stored energy cover the complete device load.", "You can now explain the qualitative architectural discontinuities in memory and power that differentiate TinyML from mobile ML."]}], "id": "v1-02-08-tinyml-ubiquitous-sensing", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-09-choosing-a-deployment-paradigm": {"title": "Choosing a Deployment Paradigm", "summary": "Apply the sequential screening logic of the four-layer deployment decision framework.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Choosing a Deployment Paradigm", "Weighing operational trade-offs across tiers", "Four screening checks for choosing a tier"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Apply the sequential screening logic of the four-layer deployment decision framework\n- Calculate the physical displacement and safety consequences of network latency in autonomous vehicle braking systems\n- Evaluate the complexity tax of deploying machine learning systems compared to traditional software heuristics\n- Evaluate the critical pitfall of selecting deployment paradigms based on model accuracy alone\n- Evaluate how Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) analysis prevents misleading paradigm selection based on compute list prices alone"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Choosing a Deployment Paradigm", "md": "An architect choosing where to run an ML feature rarely optimizes one dimension. A privacy rule may forbid cloud processing, a latency budget may forbid remote inference, a memory footprint may exceed the mobile device, and a cost target may rule out always-on edge hardware. Cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are operating envelopes for those conflicts, so selecting among them requires a unified comparison framework and a structured decision process."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Weighing operational trade-offs across tiers\n- Four screening checks for choosing a tier\n\nPay attention to: Operational comparison of the four paradigms beyond their resource envelopes; Four-layer decision screening framework (Privacy -> Latency -> Compute -> Cost); The complexity tax of ML systems compared to simple heuristics."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: What is the **complexity tax** in ML systems engineering, and when is a simple heuristic (e.g., rule-based logic or regular expressions) superior to a deep learning pipeline?", "md": "The complexity tax refers to the extensive hidden operational overhead that an ML system introduces beyond its core model code, including data validation pipelines, continuous monitoring, retraining infrastructure, GPU dependencies, and model drift management. An accuracy increase from 90% to 95% alongside a maintenance increase from 1 to 40 hours/month requires comparing the value of avoided errors with the additional operating costs and risks. Those ratios alone do not establish the better choice: a simple heuristic is preferable when the ML system's additional costs and risks outweigh the value of its improved predictions."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-09-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "In the four-layer deployment screening decision framework, why is the **Privacy** layer evaluated first, followed by **Latency**, **Compute Demand**, and **Cost**?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The decision framework sequences evaluation checks by their ability to disqualify candidates immediately: privacy and regulatory mandates can strictly prohibit sending raw data off-premises; physical latency constraints (e.g., speed of light) can make remote execution physically incapable of meeting real-time SLAs; compute/memory capacity determines whether candidate hardware can fit the workload; and finally, cost and operational complexity compare the feasible options. The option claiming privacy is a compiler flag trivializes regulatory compliance. The option alleging cloud GPU access requires upfront privacy agreements confuses commercial terms with architectural screening. The choice claiming alphabetical ordering is factually false.", "type": "single", "options": ["The layers are ordered by how rapidly a hard constraint can completely invalidate an architecture: legal/privacy rules may forbid remote transmission entirely, physical latency limits can make cloud offload impossible, compute demands eliminate undersized devices, and cost compares the remaining feasible survivors.", "Privacy is the easiest parameter to optimize in software using compiler flags, whereas cost cannot be calculated until after deployment.", "Cloud providers require privacy agreements before allowing access to GPU instances.", "The framework is ordered purely alphabetically based on traditional software engineering conventions."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: apply the sequential screening logic of the four-layer deployment decision framework."]}, {"q": "An autonomous vehicle travels at 100 km/h (~27.8 m/s). An emergency pedestrian detection system evaluates two architectures: (1) an automotive Edge ML accelerator with a 15 ms local detection latency, and (2) a Cloud ML server with 115 ms total latency (100 ms network RTT + 15 ms compute). How much additional distance does the vehicle travel before braking begins if the cloud architecture is used?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. At \\$100 km/h, the vehicle travels at frac1003.6 approx 27.78 m/s. The cloud architecture adds \\$100 ms = 0.1 s of network round-trip delay. The additional distance traveled during this delay is \\$27.78 m/s × 0.1 s approx 2.78 m (~2.8 meters). In safety-critical emergency braking, a 2.8-meter delay can be the difference between stopping safely and a catastrophic collision, demonstrating why the specified 115 ms remote path fails the chapter's 100 ms deadline. The 15 ms local detection time is only one component; the complete local response path still needs validation. The options calculating 0.28 m, 28 m, or 100 m represent arithmetic scaling errors.", "type": "single", "options": ["0.28 meters", "Approximately 2.8 meters", "Approximately 28 meters", "Exactly 100 meters"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the physical displacement and safety consequences of network latency in autonomous vehicle braking systems."]}, {"q": "In ML systems engineering, selecting a deployment paradigm based solely on benchmark model accuracy is a sound practice because hardware infrastructure can always be scaled to meet application constraints later.", "explanation": "False. Selecting a deployment paradigm based solely on accuracy without evaluating physical constraints (latency floors, battery life, thermal ceilings, memory budgets, network availability) leads to deployment failures. A high-accuracy cloud model is useless if network delay violates an emergency response SLA, and an edge model that drains a mobile battery in minutes fails regardless of benchmark accuracy. Systems constraints must be co-designed upfront.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the critical pitfall of selecting deployment paradigms based on model accuracy alone."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: What is the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) calculation, and why must ML architects evaluate TCO rather than hardware purchase price or cloud VM hourly rates alone when selecting a deployment paradigm?", "md": "Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) encompasses all direct and indirect expenses over a system's multi-year operational lifetime, including hardware CapEx, recurring energy and cooling costs, network bandwidth/egress, reliability engineering, software maintenance, and DevOps labor. Focusing solely on hardware purchase price or cloud VM rental rates leads to false economies, because operational overhead and engineering labor can dominate the listed budget, as labor does in the chapter's edge-server scenario. A deployment ranking requires matching accounting boundaries, service capacity, availability, and time horizon."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now apply the sequential screening logic of the four-layer deployment decision framework.", "You can now calculate the physical displacement and safety consequences of network latency in autonomous vehicle braking systems.", "You can now evaluate the complexity tax of deploying machine learning systems compared to traditional software heuristics.", "You can now evaluate the critical pitfall of selecting deployment paradigms based on model accuracy alone."]}], "id": "v1-02-09-choosing-a-deployment-paradigm", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-10-hybrid-architectures": {"title": "Hybrid Architectures", "summary": "Analyze how on-device wake-word detection changes cloud load by removing continuous listening traffic while leaving post-wake serving in place.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Hybrid Architectures", "Splitting work across tiers: Three integration patterns", "How hybrid tiers communicate in production"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Analyze how on-device wake-word detection changes cloud load by removing continuous listening traffic while leaving post-wake serving in place\n- Classify real-world multi-tier deployments into canonical hybrid ML integration patterns\n- Analyze the risk of training-serving skew in hybrid architectures with distributed feature pipelines\n- Explain how the train-serve split amortizes each training cycle's centralized cost across many served inferences with different local constraints\n- Sequence a progressive deployment workflow that evaluates tier-specific artifacts for one shared task against common quality goals and tier-specific resource budgets"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Hybrid Architectures", "md": "A voice interface can use a microcontroller to listen continuously for a wake word, a phone to transcribe speech, and cloud servers to interpret requests that need a larger model. Each stage has a different task and resource budget. Sending every audio sample to the cloud would add communication and radio costs; asking the microcontroller to perform every stage would exceed its compute and memory capacity. *Hybrid architectures* divide the pipeline across tiers so that each stage meets its requirements."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "In the book this section runs through:\n\n- Splitting work across tiers: Three integration patterns\n- How hybrid tiers communicate in production\n\nPay attention to: Three hybrid integration patterns: Train-Serve Split, Hierarchical Processing, Progressive Deployment; Multi-tier state synchronization and training-serving skew prevention."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: What is the economic rationale behind the **Train-Serve Split** pattern in hybrid ML architectures?", "md": "The train-serve split works because training and serving have different cost structures: training consumes substantial centralized compute and memory during each training cycle, while inference reuses the resulting trained artifact across many predictions and may require one or more forward passes per request. For example, a cloud cluster can absorb the heavy optimization cost of a training run, then edge or mobile systems can serve many local predictions without paying a network round trip each time. The practical consequence is amortization across each training cycle: the fixed cost of producing the model is spread over many served inferences, while deployment still respects local latency and privacy constraints."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-10-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Why does serving always-on voice assistant traffic entirely in the cloud create an infrastructure and economic wall, and what does on-device wake-word detection actually remove?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. The section separates two loads: continuous listening from every microphone, and the much smaller post-wake query traffic that the hybrid design still sends to the cloud. Local wake-word detection removes the continuous always-listening upload path, but it does not eliminate all cloud serving because post-wake speech and language tasks may still use larger remote models. The claim that all cloud serving disappears ignores this hybrid split, and the privacy-only answer misses the central scaling point: filtering idle audio locally changes the traffic pattern itself, not just where it is processed.", "type": "single", "options": ["On-device wake-word detection removes the need to stream every microphone continuously to the cloud, so the data-center-count estimate applies to the remaining post-wake queries rather than to constant listening traffic.", "Once wake-word detection moves on device, all cloud serving disappears because speech recognition and language understanding no longer need remote models.", "The main problem is only that encrypted audio cannot be processed efficiently in cloud accelerators, so local wake detection solves a hardware compatibility issue rather than a traffic-scaling issue.", "Wake-word detection on device matters only for privacy; it does not materially change network load because continuous listening traffic and post-wake query traffic are the same scale."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: analyze how on-device wake-word detection changes cloud load by removing continuous listening traffic while leaving post-wake serving in place."]}, {"q": "A smart agriculture system deploys soil moisture sensors on microcontrollers performing local anomaly detection, local gateway edge servers that aggregate sensor feeds across crop fields to trigger automated irrigation valves, and a central cloud system that aggregates regional data for seasonal crop yield forecasting. Which hybrid ML integration pattern does this architecture exemplify?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Hierarchical Processing organizes data and decision-making across computational tiers based on timescale and capability: TinyML nodes perform millisecond-level local anomaly detection, edge gateways aggregate multi-sensor data for sub-second local irrigation actuation, and cloud infrastructure handles macro-scale long-term analytics. Train-Serve Split focuses specifically on separating centralized training from distributed serving. Monolithic Cloud Offload routes all raw sensor telemetry directly to the cloud without intermediate edge intelligence. Linear Single-Node Pipelining refers to overlapping compute within a single host.", "type": "single", "options": ["Pure Train-Serve Split", "Monolithic Cloud Offload", "Hierarchical Processing", "Linear Single-Node Pipelining"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: classify real-world multi-tier deployments into canonical hybrid ML integration patterns."]}, {"q": "What is the primary operational failure risk in a hybrid ML system where preprocessing and feature extraction are split between client devices (mobile/edge) and centralized cloud servers?", "explanation": "The correct answer is D. In hybrid systems spanning multiple tiers, participating platforms must maintain strict synchronization of feature extraction logic, tokenizers, normalization parameters, and model versions. Discrepancies between how features are computed on edge devices versus cloud training environments produce training-serving skew, leading to silent prediction errors. The options claiming reversed transistor polarity, reduced speed of light, or register allocation failures during handshakes describe fabricated physical absurdities.", "type": "single", "options": ["Transistors in edge devices reverse polarity when receiving cloud RPC payloads.", "The speed of light in optical fiber decreases proportionally with the number of connected edge clients.", "Edge devices become unable to allocate floating-point registers during network handshakes.", "Training-serving skew, where slight discrepancies in feature extraction logic, library versions, or numerical precision between the edge client and cloud server cause silent model degradation."], "answer": 3, "hints": ["Think about: analyze the risk of training-serving skew in hybrid architectures with distributed feature pipelines."]}, {"q": "Order the steps of a Progressive Deployment evaluation for a shared task across multiple tiers:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (4) Define the shared task, required quality level, and each tier's resource budgets, (2) Establish a reference model or artifact for that task, (3) Produce tier-specific candidate artifacts for cloud, edge, mobile, or TinyML targets, (1) Measure each candidate and accept only those that satisfy both task quality and tier constraints. The sequence is necessary because progressive deployment starts by defining what success means under each tier's envelope, then setting a reference point for the shared task, then creating candidate artifacts for each tier, and only afterward evaluating which ones meet both quality and resource constraints. Measuring before candidate creation or before defining the task would leave the process without a valid object to test or a criterion for acceptance.", "type": "order", "items": ["Define the shared task, required quality level, and each tier's resource budgets", "Establish a reference model or artifact for that task", "Produce tier-specific candidate artifacts for cloud, edge, mobile, or TinyML targets", "Measure each candidate and accept only those that satisfy both task quality and tier constraints"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence a progressive deployment workflow that evaluates tier-specific artifacts for one shared task against common quality goals and tier-specific resource budgets."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now analyze how on-device wake-word detection changes cloud load by removing continuous listening traffic while leaving post-wake serving in place.", "You can now classify real-world multi-tier deployments into canonical hybrid ML integration patterns.", "You can now analyze the risk of training-serving skew in hybrid architectures with distributed feature pipelines.", "You can now explain how the train-serve split amortizes each training cycle's centralized cost across many served inferences with different local constraints."]}], "id": "v1-02-10-hybrid-architectures", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-11-what-every-tier-shares": {"title": "What Every Tier Shares", "summary": "Explain how systems optimization techniques (e.g., quantization, kernel fusion) transfer across deployment scales.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["What Every Tier Shares"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Explain how systems optimization techniques (e.g., quantization, kernel fusion) transfer across deployment scales"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § What Every Tier Shares", "md": "Quantizing weights from 32-bit floats to 8-bit integers cuts the bytes read from flash and SRAM on a milliwatt microcontroller for the exact same physical reason that it cuts HBM traffic during autoregressive decoding in a megawatt cloud cluster. Although the four deployment tiers differ by orders of magnitude in available energy, memory capacity, and compute throughput, they rest on shared systems foundations: every tier moves data through a pipeline, manages a fixed budget of compute, memory, energy, and network capacity, and maps models onto hardware through a software architecture."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "Pay attention to: Three-tier convergence of systems engineering principles across scales."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-11-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "Optimization techniques such as quantization and memory traffic reduction are mutually exclusive between tiers, meaning methods developed for TinyML microcontrollers have no applicability to cloud data center serving.", "explanation": "False. Because memory bandwidth bottlenecks and the memory wall exist across all computing scales, core systems principles transfer across tiers. Techniques such as integer quantization, kernel fusion, and memory layout tuning originally developed to fit models into microcontrollers or mobile NPUs are routinely applied to cloud LLM serving to reduce HBM traffic and lower inference serving costs.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain how systems optimization techniques (e.g., quantization, kernel fusion) transfer across deployment scales."]}]}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now explain how systems optimization techniques (e.g., quantization, kernel fusion) transfer across deployment scales."]}], "id": "v1-02-11-what-every-tier-shares", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-12-fallacies-and-pitfalls": {"title": "Fallacies and Pitfalls", "summary": "Calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup using Amdahl's law when optimizing an isolated ML component.", "source": {"chapter": "v1-02", "sections": ["Fallacies and Pitfalls", "Summary", "Summary"]}, "blocks": [{"type": "text", "md": "By the end of this lesson you should be able to:\n\n- Calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup using Amdahl's law when optimizing an isolated ML component\n- Explain why hardware-aware compilation and kernel fusion are essential for efficient edge deployment\n- Evaluate the pitfall of ignoring operational expenses and DevOps labor when calculating edge ML deployment costs\n- Explain the physical and statistical limits that prevent data quantity from guaranteeing deployment accuracy gains\n- Explain how kernel fusion reduces memory traffic and kernel launch overhead in inference pipelines"}, {"type": "from_book", "source": "Vol I, System Paradigms, § Fallacies and Pitfalls", "md": "Most deployment misconceptions share one root. Each treats a physical envelope as a software detail that a later optimization can fix. Data movement, thermal dissipation, and hardware balance can invalidate otherwise reasonable optimization choices, and each fallacy and pitfall below records a system mismatched to its operating envelope."}, {"type": "callout", "kind": "note", "title": "What the section covers", "md": "Pay attention to: Amdahl's law applied to ML pipelines and end-to-end system speedup limits; Per-target binary compilation and kernel fusion vs. generic model artifacts; Total Cost of Ownership fallacies and training data scaling limitations."}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Think first: Why does choosing an on-premises edge deployment solely because its hardware amortizes to a lower monthly compute cost than a cloud VM often result in a higher Total Cost of Ownership (TCO)?", "md": "Hardware compute cost is only one fraction of the total cost of ownership. Deploying on-premise edge fleets introduces substantial recurring operational expenses (OpEx), including distributed network engineering, on-site hardware maintenance, security patching, and DevOps reliability engineering, alongside extended development timelines. Adding these costs can erase an apparent hardware-only saving. A valid total-cost verdict requires the cloud and edge budgets to include comparable operating costs, capacity, availability, and accounting periods; the chapter's cloud-compute-only figure does not supply that matched comparison."}, {"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-12-quiz", "title": "Check your understanding", "questions": [{"q": "A smartphone camera pipeline takes 200 ms total: 100 ms for the image signal processor (ISP), 60 ms for ML scene classification, and 40 ms for postprocessing. If an engineering team optimizes the ML model to run \\$10× faster (reducing ML time from 60 ms to 6 ms), what is the overall system-level speedup?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. Prior to optimization, total latency is \\$100 ms + 60 ms + 40 ms = 200 ms. The ML model represents p = frac60200 = 0.30 (30%) of total pipeline execution time. Optimizing the ML stage by s = 10× reduces its latency to frac6010 = 6 ms, resulting in a new total latency of \\$100 ms + 6 ms + 40 ms = 146 ms. The overall system speedup is frac200 ms146 ms approx 1.37×. Even an infinite ML speedup (s = infty) would yield at most frac200140 approx 1.43× speedup because the remaining 70% of the pipeline is unoptimized. The option claiming a \\$10.00× system speedup commits the classic fallacy of assuming model-level speedup translates linearly to end-to-end system speedup. The options claiming \\$5.50× or \\$0.73× represent calculation errors.", "type": "single", "options": ["\\$10.00× speedup", "\\$1.37× speedup", "\\$5.50× speedup", "\\$0.73× speedup (system becomes slower)"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup using Amdahl's law when optimizing an isolated ML component."]}, {"q": "Why does deploying a single generic FP32 model binary across diverse edge devices (e.g., Arm CPU, Apple Neural Engine, and Google Coral Edge TPU) result in suboptimal efficiency compared to per-target compilation?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Different edge accelerators feature unique hardware architectures, such as specialized INT8 tensor systolic arrays, proprietary NPU delegates, and distinct on-chip memory hierarchies. A generic model binary fails to utilize kernel fusion (which avoids round-trip memory traffic between layers), cannot map operations to dedicated low-precision datapaths, and fails to align tensor dimensions with on-chip cache lines. The options claiming PCI Express signature violations, undefined mobile float math, or automatic OS weight deletion are entirely fabricated.", "type": "single", "options": ["Generic model binaries violate cryptographic signatures required by PCI Express buses.", "Floating-point operations are mathematically undefined on mobile CPUs.", "Generic binaries cannot exploit hardware-specific kernel fusion, dedicated fixed-point INT8 matrix datapaths, or accelerator-specific on-chip SRAM memory tiling.", "The operating system automatically deletes untuned model weights to prevent memory fragmentation."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain why hardware-aware compilation and kernel fusion are essential for efficient edge deployment."]}, {"q": "Increasing the quantity of training data is guaranteed to continuously improve deployed model performance regardless of model capacity, label noise, or domain distribution shift.", "explanation": "False. Data scaling benefits are bounded by three critical constraints: model capacity (a small model saturates and cannot absorb more information), data quality (noisy, mislabeled, or redundant data harms accuracy), and distribution alignment (adding massive out-of-domain data does not improve, and can degrade, performance on target deployment distributions).", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain the physical and statistical limits that prevent data quantity from guaranteeing deployment accuracy gains."]}]}, {"type": "reveal", "prompt": "Explain it: What is kernel fusion in ML compilers, and how does it optimize memory-bound inference pipelines?", "md": "Kernel fusion is an optimization technique where a compiler merges multiple adjacent operations in a neural network graph (such as Conv2D, BatchNorm, and ReLU) into a single executed kernel. By computing intermediate values directly in high-speed processor registers or on-chip SRAM rather than writing them back to off-chip DRAM and reading them again for the next layer, kernel fusion drastically reduces off-chip memory traffic ($D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$) and kernel launch overhead ($L_{\\text{lat}}$)."}, {"type": "summary", "points": ["You can now calculate end-to-end pipeline speedup using Amdahl's law when optimizing an isolated ML component.", "You can now explain why hardware-aware compilation and kernel fusion are essential for efficient edge deployment.", "You can now evaluate the pitfall of ignoring operational expenses and DevOps labor when calculating edge ML deployment costs.", "You can now explain the physical and statistical limits that prevent data quantity from guaranteeing deployment accuracy gains."]}], "id": "v1-02-12-fallacies-and-pitfalls", "chapter": "v1-02", "kind": "lesson", "minutes": 5}, "v1-02-99-test": {"title": "Test: System Paradigms", "kind": "test", "summary": "Check what you learned across the whole chapter.", "blocks": [{"type": "quiz", "id": "v1-02-test", "pass_score": 0.7, "questions": [{"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Silicon contract**?", "options": ["The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory.", "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier.", "Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated.", "Shipping data over a network grows with data volume while shipping the computation does not, so large datasets pull computation to where the data lives."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Silicon contract:** Each tier's hardware offers a peak compute rate, a memory bandwidth, and a fixed overhead that enter the iron law's three terms, while memory capacity and energy act as feasibility preconditions that can rule a tier out before any term is evaluated.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Silicon contract itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "Comparing a dedicated TinyML keyword spotting model (~\\$10\\ \\mu\\text{J}$ per inference) to a cloud foundation model query (~\\$1\\text{ kJ}$ per inference), by approximately what factor does energy consumption per inference differ?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. The energy ratio between a cloud LLM query (\\$1 kJ = 10^3 J) and an always-on TinyML keyword spotting inference (\\$10\\ muJ = 10^-5 J) is frac10^3 J10^-5 J = 10^8 = 100,000,000× (eight orders of magnitude). This is a scale comparison across different tasks and accounting boundaries, not a controlled hardware-efficiency comparison. TinyML lifetime still depends on inference cadence, sensing, communication, sleep power, and the complete device energy budget. The options citing 2 orders (\\$100×) or 4 orders (\\$10,000×) severely underestimate the energy gap between cloud data centers and microcontrollers. The option citing 14 orders (\\$10^14) overstates the ratio.", "type": "single", "options": ["Approximately \\$100× (2 orders of magnitude)", "Approximately \\$10,000× (4 orders of magnitude)", "Approximately \\$100{,}000{,}000\\times$ (\\$10^8$, 8 orders of magnitude)", "Approximately \\$10^14× (14 orders of magnitude)"], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: calculate the energy scale ratio between TinyML sensing and cloud LLM queries while distinguishing it from a matched efficiency comparison."]}, {"q": "In the chapter's 3-year budget comparison for an on-premises edge server deployment serving 1 million inferences per day, what is the largest component of the edge system's listed annual budget in the chapter's scenario?", "explanation": "The correct answer is B. In the chapter's illustrative edge-server budget, engineering labor (e.g., 0.1 FTE DevOps support per server/site, totaling ~\\$15,000/year) accounts for roughly 69% of the listed annual edge budget, far exceeding hardware CapEx (~\\$5,000/year), power (~\\$315/year), or cooling (~\\$95/year). Teams that focus solely on minimizing hardware purchase costs systematically underestimate the total cost of ownership. The choices identifying hardware amortization, electricity, or cooling as the dominant cost do not match this scenario. The comparison is a partial budget: cloud operating labor and edge redundancy are not priced, so it does not establish a matched total-cost ranking.", "type": "single", "options": ["GPU hardware silicon purchase price amortized over 3 years", "DevOps engineering labor required to maintain, update, and manage on-premises edge infrastructure", "Electricity costs for running the server 24/7", "Dedicated server room air-conditioning and cooling overhead"], "answer": 1, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate the listed edge-server budget to identify the dominant role of operational labor."]}, {"q": "Order the steps of the hierarchical deployment feasibility evaluation for the Smart Doorbell scenario from initial problem framing to latency verification:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (2) Define the application mission targets (person at the door, required quality, and responsiveness budget), (4) Select a candidate platform and model for the task (for example, a visual wake-word model on an ESP32-S3), (1) Check Level 1 storage fit by screening whether the visual wake-word model's weights fit in flash, (3) Check Level 2 timing constraints by evaluating per-inference latency against the interaction budget. The sequence is necessary because the budget and task define what \"fit\" means, the candidate must be chosen before it can be screened, and the scorecard checks flash before latency. Passing the flash check does not prove complete memory fit: SRAM for activations and runtime state still has to be checked separately.", "type": "order", "items": ["Check Level 1 storage fit by screening whether the visual wake-word model's weights fit in flash", "Check Level 2 timing constraints by evaluating per-inference latency against the interaction budget"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence a hierarchical feasibility screen for embedded deployment and distinguish flash screening from separate SRAM/runtime-memory validation."]}, {"q": "Increasing the quantity of training data is guaranteed to continuously improve deployed model performance regardless of model capacity, label noise, or domain distribution shift.", "explanation": "False. Data scaling benefits are bounded by three critical constraints: model capacity (a small model saturates and cannot absorb more information), data quality (noisy, mislabeled, or redundant data harms accuracy), and distribution alignment (adding massive out-of-domain data does not improve, and can degrade, performance on target deployment distributions).", "type": "truefalse", "answer": false, "hints": ["Think about: explain the physical and statistical limits that prevent data quantity from guaranteeing deployment accuracy gains."]}, {"q": "Order the steps of a Progressive Deployment evaluation for a shared task across multiple tiers:", "explanation": "The correct order is: (4) Define the shared task, required quality level, and each tier's resource budgets, (2) Establish a reference model or artifact for that task, (3) Produce tier-specific candidate artifacts for cloud, edge, mobile, or TinyML targets, (1) Measure each candidate and accept only those that satisfy both task quality and tier constraints. The sequence is necessary because progressive deployment starts by defining what success means under each tier's envelope, then setting a reference point for the shared task, then creating candidate artifacts for each tier, and only afterward evaluating which ones meet both quality and resource constraints. Measuring before candidate creation or before defining the task would leave the process without a valid object to test or a criterion for acceptance.", "type": "order", "items": ["Define the shared task, required quality level, and each tier's resource budgets", "Establish a reference model or artifact for that task", "Produce tier-specific candidate artifacts for cloud, edge, mobile, or TinyML targets", "Measure each candidate and accept only those that satisfy both task quality and tier constraints"], "hints": ["Think about: sequence a progressive deployment workflow that evaluates tier-specific artifacts for one shared task against common quality goals and tier-specific resource budgets."]}, {"q": "Why does deploying a single generic FP32 model binary across diverse edge devices (e.g., Arm CPU, Apple Neural Engine, and Google Coral Edge TPU) result in suboptimal efficiency compared to per-target compilation?", "explanation": "The correct answer is C. Different edge accelerators feature unique hardware architectures, such as specialized INT8 tensor systolic arrays, proprietary NPU delegates, and distinct on-chip memory hierarchies. A generic model binary fails to utilize kernel fusion (which avoids round-trip memory traffic between layers), cannot map operations to dedicated low-precision datapaths, and fails to align tensor dimensions with on-chip cache lines. The options claiming PCI Express signature violations, undefined mobile float math, or automatic OS weight deletion are entirely fabricated.", "type": "single", "options": ["Generic model binaries violate cryptographic signatures required by PCI Express buses.", "Floating-point operations are mathematically undefined on mobile CPUs.", "Generic binaries cannot exploit hardware-specific kernel fusion, dedicated fixed-point INT8 matrix datapaths, or accelerator-specific on-chip SRAM memory tiling.", "The operating system automatically deletes untuned model weights to prevent memory fragmentation."], "answer": 2, "hints": ["Think about: explain why hardware-aware compilation and kernel fusion are essential for efficient edge deployment."]}, {"type": "single", "q": "Which statement captures **Data locality invariant**?", "options": ["When no single tier satisfies a whole pipeline, stages go where their limiting term is cheapest through train-serve splits, hierarchical filtering and confidence cascades, and progressive deployment, at the price of keeping features, versions, and preprocessing synchronized across tiers.", "The light barrier puts a round-trip floor under any remote path, the power wall (dynamic power roughly cubic in frequency) caps sustained mobile power at a few watts, and the memory wall makes data movement a growing share of time, so cloud, edge, mobile, and TinyML are constraint regimes spanning about nine orders of magnitude in power and memory.", "A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time.", "Compute has grown faster than memory bandwidth (about 1.6x versus 1.2x per year), so data movement takes an ever larger share of time and energy at every tier."], "answer": 2, "explanation": "**Data locality invariant:** A remote path is rejected when even its lower bound (transfer time plus propagation plus remote compute) exceeds the deadline, because transmission and propagation cannot be bought down with more remote compute, and only surviving paths are compared on measured time.", "hints": ["Look for the statement whose subject is Data locality invariant itself, not a neighbouring idea."]}, {"q": "For batch-1 LLM decode in the cloud and for most edge inference, the iron-law term that dominates is memory ____ rather than peak compute, so a faster accelerator alone yields little speedup.", "explanation": "bandwidth. These workloads stream weights and activations with little reuse per byte loaded, so the data-movement term $D_{\\text{vol}}/\\text{BW}$ dominates. The productive optimizations reduce bytes moved (smaller models, lower precision, more reuse per loaded weight) rather than adding peak FLOP/s.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["bandwidth"], "hints": ["Think about: identify the dominant iron-law term for a deployment tier and the optimizations it rewards."]}, {"q": "Which statement correctly characterizes the span of power and memory capacity across the four deployment paradigms (Cloud ML, Edge ML, Mobile ML, and TinyML)?", "explanation": "The correct answer is A. Cloud ML operates at megawatt (\\$10^6\\text{ W}$) scale in data centers with terabytes (\\$10^{12}\\text{ bytes}$) of memory, whereas TinyML operates on microcontrollers consuming milliwatts (\\$10^{-3}\\text{ W}$) with kilobytes (\\$10^3\\text{ bytes}$) of RAM, establishing a nine-order-of-magnitude span in power and memory capacity. The option claiming a three-order power span severely underestimates data-center power and microcontroller power limits. The claim that Edge, Mobile, and TinyML have identical sub-5 W power ignores that edge servers typically consume 100 W to several hundred watts. The option asserting only a \\$10^3$ memory variation fails to recognize the gap between terabytes and kilobytes (\\$10^9$ ratio).", "type": "single", "options": ["Power spans approximately nine orders of magnitude from megawatts in cloud data centers to milliwatts in microcontrollers, while memory spans from terabytes to kilobytes.", "Power spans three orders of magnitude from kilowatts to watts, while memory remains in the gigabyte range across all four deployment tiers.", "Power is identical across Edge, Mobile, and TinyML tiers (all operating under 5 W), with only Cloud ML exceeding 100 W.", "Memory capacity varies by at most a factor of \\$10^3$ between cloud servers and microcontrollers, while power varies by \\$10^{12}$."], "answer": 0, "hints": ["Think about: compare the operational power and memory envelopes across the four deployment paradigms."]}, {"q": "Running biometric matching (such as Face ID) inside a dedicated on-device Secure Enclave reduces data disclosure risks by keeping mathematical representations isolated in encrypted hardware, but it does not automatically eliminate all security or privacy risks without rigorous end-to-end system design.", "explanation": "True. Hardware isolation mechanisms like the Secure Enclave restrict biometric mathematical representations to dedicated encrypted memory on-device, preventing remote transmission and unauthorized operating system access. However, overall system security and privacy still depend on the complete threat model, authentication policies, and software implementation.", "type": "truefalse", "answer": true, "hints": ["Think about: evaluate how dedicated hardware enclaves (e.g., Secure Enclave) provide biometric privacy while recognizing holistic system threat models."]}, {"q": "The historical scaling relationship under which shrinking MOSFET transistor dimensions allowed reducing voltage and current while keeping power density constant is known as ____ scaling.", "explanation": "Dennard. Dennard scaling (formulated by Robert Dennard in 1974) held that as transistors shrank, power density remained constant because operating voltage and current scaled downward proportionally. Its breakdown in the mid-2000s ended free clock frequency scaling and forced the computing industry toward multicore parallelism and domain-specific accelerators.", "type": "fill", "answer": ["Dennard"], "hints": ["Think about: explain the transistor power-scaling law whose breakdown led to domain-specific ML accelerators."]}], "mode": "test"}], "id": "v1-02-99-test", "chapter": "v1-02", "minutes": 10}});
